<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Codex Cumanicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1300</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Codex Cumanicus is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> compiled in the late 13th and early 14th centuries for Italian merchants and Franciscan missionaries working in the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. It contains a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>–Persian–Cuman glossary, Cuman riddles and Christian texts in Cuman, and is the main record of this Kipchak Turkic language. It is kept in the Marciana Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-cumanicus.jpg" alt="Codex Cumanicus" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Cumanicus_58.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Codex Cumanicus is a linguistic manual of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, designed to help Catholic missionaries communicate with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, a nomadic Turkic people. It likely developed over time and consists of a number of independent works combined into one. Mercantile, political and religious leaders, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, sought effective communication with the Cumans as early as the mid-11th century. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-city-states">Italian city-states</a> such as the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> established trade posts and colonies along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, the need for tools to learn Cuman rose sharply. The earliest parts are believed to date from the 12th or 13th century, with substantial additions made later. The copy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> is dated 11 July 1303 on folio 1r.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first part is a dictionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Persian and Cuman written in the Latin alphabet, with a column of Cuman verbs, names and pronouns glossed in Latin. It was written for the practical purpose of learning the language. The second part holds a Cuman-German dictionary and information on Cuman grammar. It was written to spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, with quotations from religious books given in Cuman translation, together with words, phrases and sentences, stories about the lives and work of religious leaders, and about 50 riddles. The codex itself calls the language <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> (tatar til).</p><p>The Cuman riddles are a crucial source for the study of early Turkic folklore. Andreas Tietze called them the earliest variants of riddle types that form a common heritage of the Turkic nations. One runs: The white yurt has no mouth; that is the egg. Another describes a place where the speaker sits as hilly and where he treads as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> bowl; the answer is the stirrup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The codex is considered one of the oldest attestations of the Crimean Tatar language and is of great importance for the history of the Kipchak and Oghuz languages, which are directly related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> steppes, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. It is kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biblioteca-marciana">Library of St Mark</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> as BNM ms Lat. Z. 549.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Codex Cumanicus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their language became the common speech of the Golden Horde and is recorded in the <b>Codex Cumanicus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cumania">Cumania</a></li></ul></section>
